    {
      "text": "On the geometry of two state models for the colored Jones polynomial — Using the ﬂow property of the R-matrix deﬁning the colored Jones polynomial, we establish a natural bijection between the set of\n\n**On the geometry of two state models for the colored Jones polynomial**\n\n*September 16, 2022*\n\n66 pp, 12 sections.\n\n**Abstract.** Using the ﬂow property of the R-matrix deﬁning the colored Jones polynomial, we establish a natural bijection between the set of states on the part arc-graph of a link projection and the set of states on a corresponding bichromatic digraph, called arc-graph, as deﬁned by Garoufalidis and Loebl [7]. We use this to give a new and essentially elementary proof for the knot state-sum formula in [7]. We will show that the state-sum contributions of states on the part arc-graph de- ﬁned by the universal R-matrix of Uq(sl(2, C)) correspond, under our bijection of sets of states, to the contributions in [7]. This will show that the two state models are in fact not essentially distinct. Our ap- proach will also extend the formula of Garoufalidis and Loebl to links. This requires some additional non-trivial observations concerning the geometry of states on the part arc-graphs.\n\n**Outline.** Front matter; 1 Introduction; 2 State models; 3 Graphs and colorings for state-sums of the c; 4 The flow lemma; 5 The R-matrix state model; 6 Proving the Garoufalidis-Loebl state-sum; 7 Evaluation of the Garoufalidis-Loebl state-s; 8 Working with cyclic ordering; 9 Weaving links; 10 Vertex ordering by braids; 11 Questions and projects",
      "lang": "en",
